\begin{quote}
\textbf{\uline{Internet of Things Labs}}

During these lab sessions, you will work in groups of three on an IoT
project. You will send data from an electronic device to the cloud via
an intermediate device, Edge.

During these sessions, you will be able to use the knowledge you have
acquired in other subjects you have studied in previous years, such as
project management, programming, AI model training, etc.

We will assume that your group is a company, and I will hire you to
carry out an IoT project. This project will have minimum requirements,
which will be evaluated at the end of the course (Below is an outline
and summary of what you will implement during the practice sessions). As
a group, you will have to manage the implementation of the project.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=5.89444in,height=2.05139in]{vertopal_5e758637c6f44a23a9d7f898a1228827/media/image1.png}

\begin{quote}
You can make a small-scale implementation of your design project. As it
may not be possible to implement everything you propose in the
theoretical design document, you can make a simulation of the system,
generate data and transmitting it from the Device to the Cloud.

\uline{Device (Sensor and PCB)}

Sensors are devices that capture events in our environment. It can be a
button, an accelerometer or a light, sound, temperature, or humidity
sensor.

They are connected to a microcontroller that is responsible of receiving
sensor data and processing them. In our case we will use Bluetooth Low
Energy (BLE) to extract the data captured by the sensor.

During the course, we lend each group a Nordic nRF52840-DK ( )

\uline{Edge (Android mobile app or Raspberry pi or Laptop)}

An application will connect to the PCB via BLE and receive the data.
These can be processed on the device or sent to a server over the
Internet.

It is proposed to use android since 70\% of mobile phones use this
operating system. In case you have an iPhone and a Mac laptop, you can
develop an application for this system.

Another option is to use an embedded platform instead of a mobile like
Edge. For example, you can use a Raspberry pi and develop BLE
communication on this platform.

\uline{Cloud}
\end{quote}

In a system where data is captured, the most important thing is to be
able to store and display

it. That is why we need a database with a back end and a system that
represents this data, for

\begin{quote}
example a graph on the web, front end.

With this data we can perform computational tasks such as prediction or
detection.

(As a Cloud you can use a commercial platform or create a server on your
own computer to

simulate it).

\textbf{\uline{Tasks:}}

• Program de PCB and send data via BLE

• Receive data from BLE in an Android app or Edge device

• Send data from Edge to Cloud

• Store data on the cloud server

• Create some ML model with the data at Edge or/and Cloud level.

• Show data to final users

\uline{Embedded system Example (Nordic nRF52)}

For PCB programming, you can use:

• Nordic provide with different tool and a plugin for VSCode to program

devices

• PlatformIO

. It is a framework that allows you to transparently program different

embedded processors without needing to know the specifics of each
manufacturer.

Allows you to use Arduino, ZephyrRTOS, and Mbed.

- \emph{\textbf{Arduino (deprecated -- Errors in BLE library since
2024)}}

- \emph{\textbf{Zephyr}}

Step by step to run bluetooth through Zephyr (v2.7) on the Nrf52 board:
\end{quote}

• Download the repository

• Unzip and go to the folder ``example/zephyr-ble-beacon'' and copy it
to your working

\begin{quote}
directory.
\end{quote}

• In visual Studio Code ``Open project'' and select the above folder.

• In the ``platform.ini'' file delete the ``particle\_xenon'' board
block.

• Compile and upload.

• In your mobile phone, using the NRFConnect app you should see a beacon
``Test beacon''.

\begin{quote}
\uline{Example Android BLE (Java):}

Java and Kotlin documentation and example)

Android applications can be used as Edge devices in the IoT
architecture. Students may implement their mobile application using
either \textbf{Kotlin} (recommended) or \textbf{Java}.

\textbf{1. Permissions Configuration ( AndroidManifest.xml )}

To prevent runtime permission issues on modern Android devices (Android
12 / API level 31+), ensure your project declares the updated BLE
permissions in the manifest:

\textless uses-permission
android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH\_SCAN"
android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" /\textgreater{}

\textless uses-permission
android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH\_CONNECT" /\textgreater{}

\textless uses-permission
android:name="android.permission.ACCESS\_FINE\_LOCATION" /\textgreater{}

\emph{(For devices running Android 11 or lower, standard legacy
Bluetooth and location permissions apply).}

\textbf{2. Technical References \& Official Documentation}

Students are expected to consult the official Android Developers
documentation to design their BLE stack (scanning, connection, and data
acquisition):

• \textbf{BLE Scanning}: Consult BluetoothLeScanner and ScanCallback
APIs to locate your PCB device using your assigned Service UUID.

• \textbf{GATT Connection \& Service Discovery}: Use BluetoothGatt and
BluetoothGattCallback to establish the connection and discover available
services and characteristics.

• \textbf{Notifications}: Enable characteristic notifications locally
and write to the Client Characteristic Configuration Descriptor (CCCD:
00002902-0000-1000-8000- 00805f9b34fb) to receive continuous sensor
updates from the PCB.

