\textbf{Project Deliverables \& Report Guidelines}

Both project reports must follow a professional technical format. As
your group operates as a

company delivering an IoT product, your documentation must clearly
detail your design

choices, custom protocol specifications, and validation steps.

\textbf{Required Report Structure}

1.\textbf{Executive Summary \& Project Overview}

• High-level description of the company, the IoT product, and the target
application/use

case.

• Summary of the project goals and system capabilities.

2.\textbf{System Architecture \& Diagram}

• \textbf{Complete Block Diagram}: A high-level visual representation
showing all three layers:

\textbf{Device (Sensors \& PCB)}, \textbf{Edge (Mobile/Gateway)}, and
Cloud (Backend/Frontend/ML).

• Identification of interfaces, data flows, and communication channels
between layers.

3.\textbf{Layer Implementation Details}

• \textbf{Device Layer}: Microcontroller board used, sensor hardware,
firmware frameworks (e.g., PlatformIO with Zephyr or Arduino), hardware
interrupts, and power management.

• \textbf{Edge Layer}: Platform choice (Android app in Kotlin/Java,
Raspberry Pi, laptop), OS version, and BLE scanning/connection
management.

• \textbf{Cloud Layer}: Backend technology, database strategy
(Time-Series, Relational, NoSQL), web UI framework, and Machine Learning
model design/purpose.

• \textbf{Libraries \& Frameworks}: Explicit list of third-party
dependencies, APIs, and libraries used at each stage.

4.\textbf{Data Structures \& Protocol Payload Specifications}

• Since each group defines its own communication strategy, you must
explicitly document your payload structures:

o \textbf{BLE Data Frame}: Precise byte breakdown of the data packet
sent over BLE (e.g., header, sensor payload fields, timestamp,
CRC/checksum).

o \textbf{Edge-to-Cloud Data Format}: Protocol used (e.g., MQTT, HTTP
REST, WebSockets) and JSON/payload schema used for data ingestion.

5.\textbf{Testing, Validation \& Results}

• \textbf{Per-Layer Testing}: Specific unit and functional tests
executed to verify each component (PCB sensor reading accuracy, BLE
connection stability, API endpoint response times).

• \textbf{End-to-End Testing}: Validation of complete data flows from
physical measurement at the PCB to visualization on the web dashboard.

• \textbf{Test Evidence}: Include graphs, logs, or screenshots
demonstrating operational stability.

\textbf{Team Assessment \& Course Feedback}

To encourage professional responsability and continuously improve the
course, the final section of the second report must include a
\textbf{Team \& Self-Assessment} along \textbf{Course Feedback}.

\textbf{1. Teamwork \& Individual Contribution}

Every group member must contribute meaningfully to the project. In this
subsection, clearly detail:

• \textbf{Task Distribution Matrix}: Provide a breakdown listing the
specific responsibilities, modules, and code components led by each
member (e.g., Firmware/PCB, Android Edge app, Backend API, Frontend UI,
ML model).

• \textbf{Self-Assessment}: Evaluate your own technical growth,
problem-solving approach, and personal commitment to the
team\textquotesingle s objectives.

• \textbf{Peer Evaluation \& Collaboration}: Describe how your team
organized its workflow (e.g., version control, task tracking, meeting
frequency), how decisions were made, and how any technical or
interpersonal challenges were resolved.

\textbf{2. Course \& Subject Feedback}

Your constructive feedback is essential for refining future editions of
this laboratory course. Please provide honest reflections on:

• \textbf{Strengths}: What aspects of the project, lab exercises,
hardware tools, or teaching methodology were most engaging and
beneficial for your learning?

• \textbf{Areas for Improvement}: What specific elements would you
modify or expand? (e.g., clarity of instructions, suggested frameworks,
hardware resources, schedule, or evaluation criteria).
\end{quote}

